From 5b15346f23d0f3283d3b99c9761795811af7308d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mesa-nvk-horizon <mesa-nvk-horizon@localhost>
Date: Mon, 7 Sep 2026 12:57:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 69/69] nouveau/vk: nvkmd_horizon, accumulate pushes across exec()
 calls
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

mesa-nvk-horizon: Phase 7 item 1 (CPU cost of submission)
Why: nvkmd_horizon_ctx_exec handed its spans straight to
 horizon_gpu_submit, so a vkQueueSubmit carrying three command buffers
 became three kickoffs. nvk_queue_submit_locked calls exec() once per
 command buffer and then signal() once for the whole submission
 (nvk_queue.c:280-325), so the number of submissions this backend makes
 is the number of command buffers the application passed — a fact about
 the loop above it, not about the work.
 .
 Each kickoff is an ioctl through the nv service, which on Horizon is an
 IPC round trip to the nvservices sysmodule. Each one also appends two
 GPFIFO entries of horizon_gpu's own: an L2_SYSMEM_INVALIDATE before the
 work, and after it a block that does WFI_SCOPE_ALL, writes L2 back and
 increments the syncpoint (horizon/submit/submit.c). Three command
 buffers therefore cost three round trips and, on the GPU, two full
 pipeline drains nothing had asked for.
 .
 The context now holds what exec() has accepted and sends it at the
 boundaries that need it: signal() — which nvkmd documents as implying
 flush() and which NVK calls at the end of every submission — plus
 flush(), sync(), wait() and bind(), and a 64-push backstop so a very
 long submission cannot leave the channel idle while the CPU walks it.
 .
 NO BARRIER WAS REMOVED OR MOVED BY HAND. horizon_gpu_submit still emits
 exactly one prologue and one fence block per call, unchanged; there are
 simply fewer calls. What changes is where those boundaries fall: at the
 end of the submission instead of between its command buffers. That is
 the boundary Vulkan defines — command buffers in one VkSubmitInfo
 execute in order with no implicit memory dependency between them, and
 every dependency the application asks for is already in the pushbuffer
 NVK recorded. The prologue moves the same way and is strictly safer
 for it: one L2 invalidate emitted after all of the CPU's writes covers
 more than three emitted between them.
 .
 What the boundaries protect. wait() sends the batch first because
 horizon_gpu_submit_waits appends to the same in-order GPFIFO, so a wait
 pushed while the batch was still on the CPU would execute ahead of work
 exec() had already accepted. bind() sends it because a bind takes
 effect when its ioctl returns, so an unbind would otherwise apply
 underneath work that had not been submitted yet. signal() sends it
 because the fence it hands out is ctx->last_fence, which names the last
 kickoff and not the batch. An `incomplete` push and its continuation
 are never split: the backstop is skipped at such a boundary, and NVK
 only ever produces the pair inside one exec() call anyway
 (nvk_cmd_buffer_push_indirect).
 .
 A failed kickoff drops the batch: the work never reached the GPU and no
 fence was ever handed out for it, so carrying it to the next boundary
 would submit, under a later fence, work the caller was already told
 failed. A batch still present at destroy() is dropped too, and said so
 loudly — submitting into a channel about to go away, whose pushbuffers
 the caller may already have freed, is the worse of the two failures.
 .
 NVK_HORIZON_BATCH_EXEC=0 restores one kickoff per exec() call, so the
 two can be compared on one console in one session. The meter counts
 exec calls, the pushes they carried and the kickoffs that went out, and
 prints the pushes-per-kickoff mean; equal exec calls and kickoffs mean
 the workload submits one command buffer at a time and this bought it
 nothing, which is a real answer about that workload.
Evidence: nvk_queue.c:280-325 is the loop that calls exec() once per
 command buffer and signal() once after them.
 horizon/submit/submit.c:180-270 is the prologue, the fence block and the
 single nvGpuChannelKickoff each submit performs. Cross-compiled clean
 for aarch64-none-elf with the project's -Wall -Wextra -Werror through
 ghcr.io/d3fau4/nx-dev; the driver-linked ELFs pass
 scripts/check-dispatch-complete.sh. NO CONSOLE HAS RUN IT, and the
 pushes-per-kickoff line is the measurement that decides whether it is
 worth anything on a given workload.
Upstream: no — nvkmd_horizon is this project's backend, and the nouveau
 backend already batches this way through its own kernel interface.
---
 .../vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon.h      |  70 ++-
 .../vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c  | 402 +++++++++++++++---
 2 files changed, 406 insertions(+), 66 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon.h b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon.h
index cf4236b..a1b104c 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon.h
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon.h
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 
 #include "c11/threads.h"
 #include "util/simple_mtx.h"
+#include "util/u_dynarray.h"
 #include "vk_sync.h"
 #include "vk_sync_timeline.h"
 
@@ -212,6 +213,25 @@ struct nvkmd_horizon_meter {
 
    uint32_t submits;
 
+   /* nvkmd_horizon_ctx_exec: how many times NVK asked for work to run,
+    * and how many pushes those calls carried between them. `submits`
+    * above is how many kickoffs actually went out.
+    *
+    * THE THREE TOGETHER ARE THE MEASUREMENT, and none of them is it
+    * alone. exec_calls/submits is how many of NVK's calls one kickoff
+    * carried; exec_spans/submits is how many GPFIFO entries that
+    * kickoff held. A workload that submits one command buffer per
+    * vkQueueSubmit has exec_calls == submits and accumulating across
+    * exec() has bought it nothing — which is a real answer about that
+    * workload and the reason these are counted separately rather than
+    * folded into one ratio. */
+   uint32_t exec_calls;
+   uint32_t exec_spans;
+   /* Of `submits`, the ones that carried at least one push. A GPU-wait
+    * batch and a fence-only signal are submissions too and would spoil
+    * the pushes-per-kickoff mean. */
+   uint32_t exec_kickoffs;
+
    /* nvkmd_horizon_ctx_wait: the waits it performed on the CPU, and the
     * ones it skipped because this channel had already ordered them. */
    uint32_t ctx_waits;
@@ -240,6 +260,20 @@ struct nvkmd_horizon_meter {
    uint64_t fence_age_ns;
 };
 
+/* How many pushes accumulate before a kickoff goes out on its own.
+ *
+ * NOT A HARDWARE LIMIT. horizon_gpu_submit takes up to
+ * GPFIFO_QUEUE_SIZE - 2 spans and reports BUSY rather than truncating,
+ * which is far more than this. What this bounds is the window in which
+ * the GPU has nothing to do because the CPU is still collecting: a
+ * submission of very many command buffers would otherwise leave the
+ * channel idle until the last one had been walked. Sixty-four is well
+ * past any submission this driver has been seen to make, so in practice
+ * the flush points below are what send a batch and this is the backstop
+ * that keeps the pathological case bounded.
+ */
+#define NVKMD_HORIZON_BATCH_SPANS 64u
+
 struct nvkmd_horizon_ctx {
    struct nvkmd_ctx base;
 
@@ -249,10 +283,36 @@ struct nvkmd_horizon_ctx {
     * carries one syncpoint increment, so this is "when everything
     * submitted so far has completed" — which is exactly what a signal
     * means.
+    *
+    * IT MOVES ON A KICKOFF AND NOT ON AN exec(). With work accumulating
+    * across exec() calls, a fence taken between the exec and the
+    * kickoff would name a submit that has not happened. Every reader of
+    * it — ctx_signal above all — goes through a kickoff first.
     */
    horizon_gpu_fence last_fence;
    bool has_fence;
 
+   /* The pushes exec() has accepted and not yet sent, as the spans
+    * horizon_gpu_submit takes. Cleared on kickoff, which keeps the
+    * allocation, so a steady state does no allocation at all.
+    *
+    * ADDRESSES AND SIZES, NEVER THE CALLER'S POINTERS. nvkmd hands
+    * exec() a `struct nvkmd_ctx_exec` array that nvk_queue.c builds in
+    * a STACK_ARRAY and frees the moment exec() returns; what survives
+    * here is the two numbers copied out of it.
+    */
+   struct util_dynarray batch;
+
+   /* True when the last push in the batch ends mid-method, so the next
+    * push has to be in the same submit as it (nvkmd.h:436). A kickoff
+    * is never taken at such a boundary. */
+   bool batch_incomplete;
+
+   /* NVK_HORIZON_BATCH_EXEC=0 turns the accumulation off and makes
+    * every exec() its own kickoff, which is what this backend did
+    * before. It is the A/B control, read once per context. */
+   bool batch_enabled;
+
    struct nvkmd_horizon_meter meter;
 };
 
@@ -376,7 +436,15 @@ void nvk_horizon_sync_set_fence(struct vk_sync *sync,
 void nvkmd_horizon_meter_init(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m,
                               horizon_gpu_channel *chan);
 void nvkmd_horizon_meter_finish(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m);
-void nvkmd_horizon_meter_submit(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m);
+/* One kickoff. `spans` is how many pushes it carried — zero for a
+ * GPU-wait batch or a fence-only signal, which are submissions but not
+ * work. */
+void nvkmd_horizon_meter_submit(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m,
+                                uint32_t spans);
+/* One exec() call, and the pushes it carried. Counted where NVK asks,
+ * not where the kickoff happens, so the two can be compared. */
+void nvkmd_horizon_meter_exec(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m,
+                              uint32_t spans);
 /* One wait resolved by the host engine rather than by this thread. */
 void nvkmd_horizon_meter_ctx_wait_gpu(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m,
                                       uint32_t count);
diff --git a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c
index b87fb2a..ca0bcba 100644
--- a/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c
+++ b/src/nouveau/vulkan/nvkmd/horizon/nvkmd_horizon_ctx.c
@@ -5,8 +5,6 @@
 
 #include "nvkmd_horizon.h"
 
-#include "util/stack_array.h"
-
 #include "vk_log.h"
 #include "vk_sync.h"
 
@@ -86,11 +84,30 @@ nvkmd_horizon_meter_report_locked(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m,
                 "wait blocked", (void *)m->chan, window_ms);
    }
 
+   /* THE BATCHING LINE, and it is three numbers because no one of them
+    * answers the question. `exec call(s)` is how many times NVK asked
+    * for work; `push(es)` is how many GPFIFO entries those calls
+    * carried; `kickoff(s)` is how many submissions actually went out
+    * with work in them. Equal exec calls and kickoffs means this
+    * workload submits one command buffer at a time and accumulating
+    * across exec() has bought it nothing at all. */
+   if (m->exec_calls > 0) {
+      mesa_logi("nvkmd_horizon: channel %p over %" PRIu64 " ms: %" PRIu32
+                " exec call(s) carrying %" PRIu32 " push(es) went out as %"
+                PRIu32 " kickoff(s), mean %" PRIu32 " push(es) per kickoff",
+                (void *)m->chan, window_ms, m->exec_calls, m->exec_spans,
+                m->exec_kickoffs,
+                m->exec_kickoffs > 0 ? m->exec_spans / m->exec_kickoffs : 0);
+   }
+
    /* `enabled` and `chan` survive; everything the window accumulated
     * does not, so the next line is about the next second rather than
     * about the whole session. */
    m->window_start_ns = 0;
    m->submits = 0;
+   m->exec_calls = 0;
+   m->exec_spans = 0;
+   m->exec_kickoffs = 0;
    m->ctx_waits = 0;
    m->ctx_waits_skipped = 0;
    m->ctx_wait_ns = 0;
@@ -129,7 +146,7 @@ nvkmd_horizon_meter_finish(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m)
 }
 
 void
-nvkmd_horizon_meter_submit(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m)
+nvkmd_horizon_meter_submit(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m, uint32_t spans)
 {
    if (m == NULL || !m->enabled)
       return;
@@ -140,6 +157,29 @@ nvkmd_horizon_meter_submit(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m)
    if (m->window_start_ns == 0)
       m->window_start_ns = now_ns;
    m->submits++;
+   /* Only a submission that carried work counts towards the pushes-per-
+    * kickoff mean. A GPU-wait batch and a fence-only signal are
+    * submissions too, and folding them in would make the mean look
+    * worse the more of them a frame needed. */
+   if (spans > 0)
+      m->exec_kickoffs++;
+   nvkmd_horizon_meter_report_locked(m, now_ns, false);
+   simple_mtx_unlock(&m->mutex);
+}
+
+void
+nvkmd_horizon_meter_exec(struct nvkmd_horizon_meter *m, uint32_t spans)
+{
+   if (m == NULL || !m->enabled)
+      return;
+
+   const uint64_t now_ns = os_time_get_nano();
+
+   simple_mtx_lock(&m->mutex);
+   if (m->window_start_ns == 0)
+      m->window_start_ns = now_ns;
+   m->exec_calls++;
+   m->exec_spans += spans;
    nvkmd_horizon_meter_report_locked(m, now_ns, false);
    simple_mtx_unlock(&m->mutex);
 }
@@ -220,6 +260,32 @@ nvkmd_horizon_ctx_destroy(struct nvkmd_ctx *_ctx)
 {
    struct nvkmd_horizon_ctx *ctx = nvkmd_horizon_ctx(_ctx);
 
+   /* A BATCH LEFT HERE IS DROPPED, LOUDLY, AND NOT SENT.
+    *
+    * It should be impossible: signal(), flush() and sync() all send the
+    * batch, and NVK ends every submission with a signal. If one is here
+    * anyway, submitting it would push work into a channel that is about
+    * to be destroyed and whose pushbuffers the caller may already have
+    * freed — the worse of the two failures. Dropping it cannot make a
+    * fence lie either, because a fence is only handed out by a kickoff
+    * and this work never had one.
+    *
+    * So it is reported, in the same spirit as the busy channel below: a
+    * non-empty batch at destroy time is a driver bug and should be
+    * visible rather than absorbed.
+    */
+   const uint32_t stranded =
+      util_dynarray_num_elements(&ctx->batch, horizon_gpu_cmd_span);
+   if (stranded > 0) {
+      mesa_loge("nvkmd_horizon: channel %p destroyed with %" PRIu32
+                " push(es) never submitted; they are dropped. Something "
+                "called exec() without a signal(), flush() or sync() "
+                "after it.", (void *)ctx->chan, stranded);
+   }
+   /* Freed on both paths, including the one below that deliberately
+    * leaks the context: nothing reads the batch after this point. */
+   util_dynarray_fini(&ctx->batch);
+
    /* First, and before the channel goes away: the last window is
     * reported here, and the error path below returns without freeing
     * the context, so anything left until after it would be lost in
@@ -243,6 +309,12 @@ nvkmd_horizon_ctx_destroy(struct nvkmd_ctx *_ctx)
    FREE(ctx);
 }
 
+/* Sends whatever exec() has accumulated. Defined below, beside exec();
+ * declared here because every boundary that has to send a batch comes
+ * first in this file. */
+static VkResult nvkmd_horizon_ctx_kickoff(struct nvkmd_horizon_ctx *ctx,
+                                          struct vk_object_base *log_obj);
+
 /* CPU-waits the syncs a GPU-side wait could not carry, and meters them.
  * Used both for the kinds that never qualify and as the fallback when
  * the ring is full or the submit is refused.
@@ -333,8 +405,10 @@ nvkmd_horizon_ctx_flush_gpu_waits(struct nvkmd_horizon_ctx *ctx,
       /* horizon_gpu_submit_waits() is a real horizon_gpu_submit() under
        * the hood, so it counts as one for the same reason the exec and
        * fence-only signal paths call this: leaving it out would make the
-       * meter's submit(s) total silently omit every GPU-wait batch. */
-      nvkmd_horizon_meter_submit(&ctx->meter);
+       * meter's submit(s) total silently omit every GPU-wait batch. It
+       * carries no pushes, so it is not one of the kickoffs the
+       * pushes-per-kickoff mean is over. */
+      nvkmd_horizon_meter_submit(&ctx->meter, 0);
       return VK_SUCCESS;
    }
 
@@ -361,6 +435,21 @@ nvkmd_horizon_ctx_wait(struct nvkmd_ctx *_ctx,
 {
    struct nvkmd_horizon_ctx *ctx = nvkmd_horizon_ctx(_ctx);
 
+   /* ANYTHING exec() IS STILL HOLDING GOES FIRST.
+    *
+    * horizon_gpu_submit_waits appends the host engine's acquire to this
+    * same GPFIFO, and a GPFIFO runs in order — so a wait pushed while
+    * the batch is still on the CPU would execute *before* work that was
+    * handed to exec() earlier. That reorders nothing today, because NVK
+    * waits at the start of a submission and execs after it, so the batch
+    * is empty here every time. It is done anyway: "the batch happens to
+    * be empty" is a property of the caller, and the ordering this
+    * preserves is a property of the interface.
+    */
+   VkResult flushed = nvkmd_horizon_ctx_kickoff(ctx, log_obj);
+   if (flushed != VK_SUCCESS)
+      return flushed;
+
    /* THE WAIT HAPPENS ON THE HOST ENGINE, NOT ON THIS THREAD.
     *
     * A wait on another channel's fence used to block the calling thread
@@ -448,6 +537,120 @@ nvkmd_horizon_ctx_wait(struct nvkmd_ctx *_ctx,
    return nvkmd_horizon_ctx_flush_gpu_waits(ctx, &batch);
 }
 
+/* Sends whatever exec() has accumulated, or does nothing when there is
+ * nothing to send.
+ *
+ * WHY THIS EXISTS AT ALL. exec() used to hand its spans straight to
+ * horizon_gpu_submit, so a vkQueueSubmit carrying three command buffers
+ * became three kickoffs. Each kickoff is an ioctl through the nv
+ * service — an IPC round trip — and each one also appends two GPFIFO
+ * entries of its own: an L2_SYSMEM_INVALIDATE before the work and, after
+ * it, a block that does WFI_SCOPE_ALL, writes L2 back and increments the
+ * syncpoint (horizon/submit/submit.c). So three command buffers cost
+ * three round trips and, on the GPU, two full pipeline drains that
+ * nothing had asked for.
+ *
+ * WHAT MOVED, AND WHAT DID NOT. No barrier was removed or relocated.
+ * horizon_gpu_submit still emits exactly one prologue and one fence
+ * block per call, unchanged; there are simply fewer calls. The
+ * boundaries that used to fall between two command buffers of one
+ * submission now fall at the end of the submission, which is the
+ * boundary Vulkan actually defines: command buffers in one
+ * VkSubmitInfo execute in order with no implicit memory dependency
+ * between them, and every dependency the application does ask for is
+ * already in the pushbuffer NVK recorded. The prologue moves the same
+ * way and is strictly safer for it — one L2 invalidate emitted after
+ * all of the CPU's writes covers more than three emitted between them.
+ *
+ * A FAILED KICKOFF DROPS THE BATCH. The work never reached the GPU and
+ * no fence was ever handed out for it, so nothing can be waiting on it;
+ * carrying it forward to the next boundary would submit, under a later
+ * fence, work the caller has already been told failed.
+ */
+static VkResult
+nvkmd_horizon_ctx_kickoff(struct nvkmd_horizon_ctx *ctx,
+                          struct vk_object_base *log_obj)
+{
+   const uint32_t n =
+      util_dynarray_num_elements(&ctx->batch, horizon_gpu_cmd_span);
+   if (n == 0)
+      return VK_SUCCESS;
+
+   const horizon_gpu_cmd_span *spans =
+      util_dynarray_begin(&ctx->batch);
+
+   horizon_gpu_fence fence;
+   horizon_gpu_result res =
+      horizon_gpu_submit(ctx->chan, spans, n,
+                         HORIZON_GPU_SUBMIT_DEFAULT, &fence);
+
+   if (res.status != HORIZON_GPU_OK) {
+      /* Name every address this submit handed the GPU, and say where it
+       * sits in our own map.
+       *
+       * The reason this is here and not left to be deduced: an MMU fault
+       * is reported by nvgpu's error notifier as a *type* and nothing
+       * else, and libnx exposes no ioctl for the faulting address, so
+       * the kernel will never tell us which address was bad. What we can
+       * say is which addresses we submitted and whether each one was
+       * mapped — and "0x… is inside VA 0x… but NOTHING IS BOUND there"
+       * is the whole diagnosis, on the spot, instead of a console round
+       * trip per hypothesis.
+       *
+       * The batch is what is named, not one exec() call's execs: with
+       * accumulation the failing submission may hold several calls'
+       * pushes, and the caller cannot tell which push was whose from a
+       * subset.
+       */
+      for (uint32_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+         char where[160];
+         nvkmd_horizon_describe_addr(nvkmd_horizon_dev(ctx->base.dev),
+                                     spans[i].gpu_va, where, sizeof(where));
+         mesa_loge("nvkmd_horizon: channel %p push %" PRIu32 "/%" PRIu32
+                   ": 0x%" PRIx64 " + 0x%" PRIx32 " bytes — %s",
+                   (void *)ctx->chan, i, n, spans[i].gpu_va,
+                   spans[i].num_dwords * 4u, where);
+      }
+
+      nvkmd_horizon_dump_va_map(nvkmd_horizon_dev(ctx->base.dev), 64);
+
+      util_dynarray_clear(&ctx->batch);
+      ctx->batch_incomplete = false;
+
+      return vk_errorf(log_obj, nvkmd_horizon_result(res),
+                       "horizon_gpu_submit(%" PRIu32 " spans) on channel %p "
+                       "failed: status %d, nv 0x%08" PRIx32,
+                       n, (void *)ctx->chan, (int)res.status, res.nv);
+   }
+
+   /* ONE THREAD PER CONTEXT, which the batch now depends on rather than
+    * merely benefits from. It was already true and already relied on —
+    * ctx->last_fence and ctx->has_fence are plain fields with no lock
+    * around them — but a fence that two threads race to overwrite is
+    * one wrong fence, while a dynarray two threads grow at once is a
+    * corrupt array. NVK gives each context one user: queue->exec_ctx is
+    * touched only from vk_queue's submit path, and the upload queue
+    * holds its own mutex across every call into its context
+    * (nvk_upload_queue.c). Written down because the cost of that
+    * stopping being true went up here.
+    */
+
+   /* Cleared, not freed: util_dynarray_clear keeps the allocation, so a
+    * steady state allocates nothing. */
+   util_dynarray_clear(&ctx->batch);
+   ctx->batch_incomplete = false;
+
+   ctx->last_fence = fence;
+   ctx->has_fence = true;
+   nvkmd_horizon_meter_submit(&ctx->meter, n);
+
+   /* No wait here. The submit is asynchronous and stays that way:
+    * rejected design 6 is a property held from the first line, not
+    * retrofitted.
+    */
+   return VK_SUCCESS;
+}
+
 static VkResult
 nvkmd_horizon_ctx_exec(struct nvkmd_ctx *_ctx,
                        struct vk_object_base *log_obj,
@@ -459,6 +662,8 @@ nvkmd_horizon_ctx_exec(struct nvkmd_ctx *_ctx,
    if (exec_count == 0)
       return VK_SUCCESS;
 
+   nvkmd_horizon_meter_exec(&ctx->meter, exec_count);
+
    /* ONE SUBMIT, NOT A RUN OF THEM.
     *
     * An earlier version of this split the execs into runs whenever
@@ -482,14 +687,11 @@ nvkmd_horizon_ctx_exec(struct nvkmd_ctx *_ctx,
     * test again, so it kept going. Found by reading, not by running:
     * NVK is unlikely to emit that many, which is what would have made
     * it a bad bug rather than an obvious one.
-    *
-    * horizon_gpu_submit takes any number of spans and reports BUSY on a
-    * full ring rather than truncating, so there is nothing to cap.
     */
-   STACK_ARRAY(horizon_gpu_cmd_span, spans, exec_count);
-   if (spans == NULL)
-      return vk_error(log_obj, VK_ERROR_OUT_OF_HOST_MEMORY);
 
+   /* VALIDATED BEFORE ANYTHING IS ACCUMULATED, so a refused call leaves
+    * the batch exactly as it was and the pushes already in it are still
+    * sent by the next boundary. */
    for (uint32_t i = 0; i < exec_count; i++) {
       /* A GPFIFO entry counts dwords, and nvkmd counts bytes. A size
        * that is not a multiple of four would silently lose the last
@@ -499,7 +701,6 @@ nvkmd_horizon_ctx_exec(struct nvkmd_ctx *_ctx,
        * here instead, where the cause is still in view.
        */
       if (execs[i].size_B % 4 != 0) {
-         STACK_ARRAY_FINISH(spans);
          return vk_errorf(log_obj, VK_ERROR_UNKNOWN,
                           "nvkmd_horizon: push %" PRIu32 " is %" PRIu32
                           " bytes, not a whole number of dwords",
@@ -523,70 +724,74 @@ nvkmd_horizon_ctx_exec(struct nvkmd_ctx *_ctx,
        * exists to prevent, so it should cover all three doors, not one.
        */
